<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as atomic bombing · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The atomic bombings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> were the attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on two Japanese cities in August 1945, the only uses of nuclear weapons in war. They killed tens of thousands of people immediately and many more afterwards. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-declaration-of-war-on-japan">Soviet declaration of war</a>, they led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki.jpg" alt="Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George R. Caron / Charles Levy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_bombing_of_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission">nuclear fission</a> in 1938 made an atomic bomb a theoretical possibility, and fears that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would build one first prompted the Einstein-Szilard letter to Roosevelt in 1939. The work was placed under the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>, eventually directed by Major General Leslie R. Groves. By July 1945 it had produced two types of bomb: Little Boy, an enriched uranium gun-type weapon, and Fat Man, a plutonium implosion-type weapon.</p><p>In the final year of the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> prepared for a costly invasion of the Japanese mainland, after a conventional bombing and firebombing campaign that devastated 64 Japanese cities. The 509th Composite Group, commanded by Colonel Paul Tibbets, was trained and then deployed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a> of 26 July 1945 demanded unconditional surrender, the alternative being prompt and utter destruction, and the Japanese government ignored it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The United Kingdom's consent was obtained, as the Quebec Agreement required, and on 25 July General Thomas T. Handy issued orders for atomic bombs to be used on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, chosen as large urban areas that also held significant military facilities. An estimated 40,000 Japanese military personnel were stationed in Hiroshima. Nagasaki was a large seaport with shipbuilding and arms industries.</p><p>A Little Boy was dropped on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> on 6 August 1945, and a Fat Man on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> three days later. Until 9 August Japan's war council still insisted on four conditions for surrender, and the cabinet, which had to decide unanimously, reached no decision that day. Japan announced its surrender on 15 August, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s declaration of war and invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and signed the instrument of surrender on 2 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The bombings killed 150,000 to 246,000 people, most of them civilians. An estimated 90,000 to 166,000 died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and 60,000 to 80,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> within two to four months, roughly half on the first day, the rest from burns, radiation sickness and other injuries. The survivors are called hibakusha, and the Japanese government has recognised about 650,000 people as such. The Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum opened in 1955.</p><p>The bombings remain the first and only use of nuclear weapons in an armed conflict, and scholars still debate their justification. Supporters argue they ended the war with minimal casualties; critics argue they were unnecessary and a war crime. In 1963 the District Court of Tokyo, in Ryuichi Shimoda v. The State, held that the bombings were illegal under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> of the time, as indiscriminate bombardment of undefended cities, but denied the appellants compensation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>atomic bombing</b> of Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August, combined with the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August and the rapid destruction of the Kwantung Army in Manchuria, finally gave Emperor Hirohito the external shock he needed to override institutional resistance to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>atomic bombings</b> of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, where many Korean labourers were among the victims, and the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, Japan surrendered on 15 August 1945, ending 35 years of colonial rule; Japanese troops remained in the south until mid-September.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Coming between the <b>atomic bombings</b>, it helped persuade Japan's leaders to surrender.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan surrendered unconditionally in 1945 after the <b>atomic bombings</b> of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and was placed under Allied occupation led by the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/japan">Japan</a></li></ul></section>
